--TEST--
`Core\Uuid::fromBytes` reads sixteen octets and refuses every other width, naming the width it was handed — spec § 11, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A length is the whole of what this member refuses: every 128-bit pattern is
// a UUID, RFC 9562's nil and max included, so the bound is asserted on both
// sides — the last width below sixteen, sixteen itself, and the first above
// it. A member that took a short buffer would answer a UUID the caller never
// handed it, which is the failure a case reading only the happy path misses.
class Probe {
    // The UUID `$b` spells, or `refused` — every refusal here is a width.
    public static function reads(bytes $b): string {
        try {
            return Core\Uuid::fromBytes($b)->toString();
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return "refused";
        }
    }
}

echo Probe::reads(Core\Bytes::fill(15, 0)), "\n";
echo Probe::reads(Core\Bytes::fill(16, 0)), "\n";
echo Probe::reads(Core\Bytes::fill(17, 0)), "\n";
echo Probe::reads(Core\Bytes::fill(0, 0)), "\n";

// All ones is the far end of the *value* space rather than of the width, and
// it is a UUID for the same reason the nil one is — `$uuid->toBytes` writes it
// back out unchanged, which is what says nothing here reads a version nibble.
echo Probe::reads(Core\Bytes::fill(16, 255)), "\n";
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Uuid::fromBytes(Core\Bytes::fill(16, 255))->toBytes()), "\n";

// The message names the width, because that is the fact the caller does not
// already have: a buffer of the wrong width came from a column, a frame or a
// slice that is off by something, and the count is what says which.
try {
    Core\Uuid::fromBytes(Core\Bytes::fill(15, 0));
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
refused
00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
refused
refused
ffffffff-ffff-ffff-ffff-ffffffffffff
ffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff
Core\Uuid::fromBytes(): a UUID is sixteen bytes, and this buffer is 15
